WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.837
Trajectory review: reading what the agent did.

2
00:00:03.117 --> 00:00:04.952
Numbers tell you that something failed.

3
00:00:05.232 --> 00:00:09.221
Transcripts tell you why, and whether the agent or the eval is at fault.

4
00:00:09.501 --> 00:00:28.168
Anthropic’s guide calls reading transcripts a critical skill, and Hamel Husain’s evals FAQ suggests a structure for agents: find the first step where things went wrong, and tally those transitions across failed runs in a transition failure matrix (last good step against first failed step).

5
00:00:28.448 --> 00:00:33.056
Early failures cascade, so the most common early cell is usually the best fix.

6
00:00:33.336 --> 00:00:37.389
For a tool-using agent, read each failed transcript with four questions.

7
00:00:37.669 --> 00:00:39.568
Which tool, and was it the right one?

8
00:00:39.848 --> 00:00:47.315
Many failures are selection errors that a better description or a smaller tool set fixes (Studio 1, lesson 11).

9
00:00:47.595 --> 00:00:48.939
Were the arguments right?

10
00:00:49.219 --> 00:00:53.208
Wrong ids, wrong date ranges, a missing pagination cursor.

11
00:00:53.488 --> 00:00:55.067
How did it handle the result?

12
00:00:55.347 --> 00:00:58.781
Did it notice an error, a truncated list, or an empty page?

13
00:00:59.061 --> 00:01:00.661
Did it stop at the right time?

14
00:01:00.941 --> 00:01:10.584
Agents fail by stopping early (declaring victory after one page of issues) and by not stopping (re-reading the same issue until the step budget runs out).

15
00:01:10.864 --> 00:01:18.864
Keep a short written note per failure, as in Studio 3’s error analysis, and let the notes, not your hunches, choose the next change.

16
00:01:19.144 --> 00:01:26.995
Then rerun several trials per scenario, because one passing trial after a fix proves little for a non-deterministic agent.
